==Estonian==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|et|fiu-fin-pro|*halpa}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|et}} {{m|gem-pro|*salwaz}}. Cognates include Finnish {{m|fi|halpa||cheap}}, more distantly English {{m|en|sallow}}.

===Adjective===
{{et-adj|halva|halba}}

# [[bad]]

====Declension====
{{rfinfl|et|adjective}}

====Antonyms====
* {{l|et|hea}}

----

==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|halb}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*halbaz}}. Compare Dutch {{m|nl|half}}, English {{m|en|half}}, Danish {{m|da|halv}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|De-halb.ogg|Audio|lang=de}}

===Adjective===
{{de-adj|-}}

# [[half]], [[halfway]]
#: {{ux|de|Er ist seit einem '''halben''' Monat nicht mehr hier gewesen.|He hasn’t been here for '''half''' a month.}}
#* '''2013''', Frederick Forsyth, translated from English by Tom Knoth, ''Der Schakal. Thriller'', Piper Verlag:
#*: Die werden '''halb''' Frankreich alarmiert haben.“ [accusative neuter of the strong declension]

====Declension====
{{de-decl-adj-notcomp|halb}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|de|halbieren}}
* {{l|de|Halbblut}}
* {{l|de|Halbkugel}}
* {{l|de|Halbschlaf}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|de|Hälfte}}

===Adverb===
{{de-adv}}

# a [[half-hour]] before, thirty minutes before {{qualifier|used with the number of the following hour}}
#: {{ux|de|Wir treffen uns um '''halb''' zwei.|We’re meeting at one-thirty.}}

